\section{Overview}

The Berzerk video subsystem comprises three interrelated pieces of hardware:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Video RAM} (8~KB): a bitmap framebuffer at 0x4000--0x5FFF, 32~bytes
  per scanline, one bit per pixel, 256$\times$256 pixels (224 visible lines).
\item \textbf{Color RAM} (2~KB): per-cell color attributes at 0x8000--0x87FF.  Each
  byte covers 8 pixels (same span as one VRAM byte), split into two 4-pixel
  nibbles.
\item \textbf{Magic RAM} (0x6000--0x7FFF): not separate memory.  Writes pass through
  two 74181 4-bit ALU chips that shift, combine, and collision-detect sprite
  data against existing VRAM content.  Reads pass through to underlying VRAM.
\end{itemize}

The MAME source \texttt{berzerk.cpp} is the authoritative reference for all
behavior in this module.  The mode line of both 74181 ALUs is permanently
tied high (Logic mode, M=1) via a call to [[mode\_w(1)]] at video start.
This means only the 16 Boolean functions are available; arithmetic carry is
never used.

