\section{Data Structures}

\subsection{Magic RAM Control}

The control byte written to I/O port 0x4B configures the 74181 ALU
select lines (bits 7--4), the mirror flag (bit 3), and the shift count
(bits 2--0).  The [[last\_shift\_data]] field latches the low 7~bits of
the most recent Magic RAM write for the two-byte shift register.  The
[[intercept]] flag is a one-way latch: it starts at 1 (no collision) and
clears to 0 when any Magic RAM write finds overlapping set bits between
the shifted sprite data and existing VRAM content.

<<magic ram control struct>>=
struct MagicRAMControl {
    uint8_t control = 0;       // port 0x4B value
    uint8_t last_shift_data = 0;
    uint8_t intercept = 1;     // 1=no collision, 0=collision detected
};
@

\subsection{Video State}

All video-related state is collected here so it can be owned by
[[BerzerkMemory]] and inspected during testing or cosimulation.

The [[vram]] and [[color\_ram]] arrays are the backing store for the
program-space regions 0x4000--0x5FFF and 0x8000--0x87FF.

The [[framebuffer]] holds the rendered RGBA output for one frame.  It is
256$\times$256 pixels even though only 224 scanlines are visible.  Lines
0x00--0x1F correspond to the vblank region and are always black.

The [[v256]] flag tracks whether the beam is in the vblank region.  The
game reads it via port 0x4E.

<<video state struct>>=
struct VideoState {
    std::array<uint8_t, 0x2000> vram;
    std::array<uint8_t, 0x0800> color_ram;
    MagicRAMControl magic;
    bool v256 = false;
    std::array<uint32_t, 256 * 256> framebuffer;

    VideoState() {
        vram.fill(0);
        color_ram.fill(0);
        framebuffer.fill(0);
    }
};
@

\section{Palette}

16 colors from 4 bits: R (bit 0), G (bit 1), B (bit 2), I (bit 3).
When the intensity bit is clear, the base values are attenuated.  We
use the standard resistor-network approximation (intensity adds 0x55 to
each active channel, base is 0xAA).  Color 0 is always black.

RGBA is packed as 0xRRGGBBAA (A=0xFF) in big-endian uint32\_t.  This is
arbitrary since SDL will reinterpret it at blit time, and we are not
adding SDL in this phase.

<<palette declaration>>=
void init_palette(uint32_t palette[16]);
@

\section{Frame Rendering}

The [[render\_frame]] function converts VRAM + Color RAM into an RGBA
framebuffer.  The algorithm follows MAME's [[screen\_update]] exactly:
iterate over every byte in VRAM, fetch the color byte, and produce 8
pixels per iteration.  The left 4 pixels use the high nibble color, the
right 4 pixels use the low nibble color.

<<render frame declaration>>=
void render_frame(VideoState& video, const uint32_t palette[16]);
@

\section{Magic RAM Write}

Writing to program addresses 0x6000--0x7FFF triggers the Magic RAM
pipeline: shift register, optional bit reversal, collision detection,
74181 ALU combine, active-low inversion, and VRAM store.

The [[offset]] parameter is relative to VRAM base (0x0000--0x1FFF).

<<magic ram write declaration>>=
void magic_ram_write(VideoState& video, uint16_t offset, uint8_t data);
@

\section{Read Intercept}

Reading port 0x4E returns the collision status in bit 7 and the V256
flag in bit 0.  The intercept flip-flop is inverted: a value of 0x80
in bit 7 means collision was detected.

<<read intercept declaration>>=
uint8_t read_intercept(const VideoState& video);
@

